$DIR/main.p:7:3: cannot store &y in p, which outlives y
    &y is a pointer to y. y is a local variable of the function literal
    (declared at main.p:6). The function literal's frame is reclaimed each time
    the literal returns, but p belongs to the function around it and lives
    longer.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value instead of a pointer to it, changing the type of p to match.
    This copies y, or moves it if it is an owner:
        p = y
    Or allocate y and store its owner, an owned[int], which moves into p:
        p = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
